  "query_preview": "..\n\nA very dry air mass is in place over Southeast Michigan, as the 00Z \nDTX sounding checked in with a PW value of 0.19 inches, a 35 C \ndewpoint depression at 700 mb, and a 45 C dewpoint depression at 850 \nmb. Thus, most of today should be dry, though an aggressive moisture \nsurge takes place between 18Z and 00Z as a 65+ knot easterly jet in \nthe 850 to 700 mb layer lifts into the eastern Great Lakes. Latest \nwater vapor imagery shows a large, negatively tilted trough axis \neast of the Mississippi River Valley with a deepening surface low \njust off the Carolina coast. The 500 mb low center is progged to \nswing through Tennessee today and through West Virginia tonight. \nThus, it looks like we will be dealing with double-barreled low \npressure centers or an elongated west-to-east low later today into \nthis evening, keeping the deep/pseudo-tropical moisture (PW values > \n1.5 inches) along the Eastern Seaboard. Still, sufficient moisture \nwill extend westward into the central Great Lakes as PW values reach \nand exceed 0.75 inches, which is on the high end for late November \nstandards. With excellent FGEN forcing lifting through this evening \nand deformation lingering through most of Friday, precipitation \namounts are still expected to reach near 1 inch in many locations, \nwith the Thumb region still primed to reach and exceed that mark.\n\nThe 00Z DTX sounding indicated a warm 850 mb temp of 5 C, though \nwith the abundant dry air, a good deal of wet-bulb cooling will take \nplace. Increasing low-level easterly winds off Lake Erie will warm \nand accelerate the moistening of the low levels, sending dewpoints \ninto the upper 30s to around 40 degrees for the south half of the \nCWA. Thus, there is not much concern for snow this afternoon into \nthe evening hours. Modest wrap-around cold advection on the back \nside of the system will cool the column tonight into Friday as winds \nback to north-northwest, making it possible for snow to mix in \nmainly along and west of U.S. 23. However, snow still looks hard-\npressed to accumulate with surface temps and dewpoints well into the \n30s alongside wet ground conditions. If anything, it appears the NBM \nhas trended a bit milder, indicating less of a snow chance.\n\nThe strength of the low-level jet and pressure gradient today will \nsupport northeast wind gusts of 30 to 40 mph, per local \nprobabilistic guidance. Precipitation drag should prevent gusts from \ngetting much stronger than that, with the highest winds near the \nshoreline areas. Northwest winds on the back side Friday should come \nin a bit lower due to the lack of strong cold advection and an \nunderwhelming (~15 mb) 6-hr pressure rise/fall couplet tracking \nthrough.\n\nShortwave ridging and surface high pressure building into the Great \nLakes will provide dry and tranquil conditions for the first half of \nthe weekend, with daytime highs moderating back into the mid to \nupper 40s on Saturday under southwest return flow. By Sunday, \nattention turns to a progressive northern-stream shortwave tracking \nacross the northern Great Lakes and a separate southern-stream wave \nlifting out of the Plains/Midwest. Current guidance favors these two \ndisturbances remaining largely separate and unphased as they cross \nthe region. Without constructive phasing to drive deeper \ncyclogenesis or prolonged moisture transport, forcing remains \nprogressive supporting just a quick shot of rain showers late Sunday\nbefore drier air returns behind the departing cold front.",
